# CODEOWNERS -- read by tedium from master (use_codeowners = true in
# tedium.toml): a PR touching these paths needs an approving review from
# an owner before the bot will land it, and the ruleset's Copilot review
# does not count. Below the catch-all are the files that decide what
# may merge.

# Catch-all (9atatimer/tds-internal#285): this repo's contents run on the owner's laptop at
# session start, so every landing needs the owner's approval. tedium
# enforces it on its path; the Landing ruleset's code-owner rule enforces
# it on GitHub's merge button, so the agents' identity (9atatimer-bot,
# push) cannot land a change alone. Agents open PRs as that identity so
# the owner can approve them (GitHub refuses self-approval). Listed
# first: last match wins, and every narrower line below names the same
# owner.
*                            @9atatimer

tedium.toml          @9atatimer
CODEOWNERS           @9atatimer
.github/workflows/   @9atatimer
# Decides which dist-ci jobs a PR runs; a wrong rule skips a suite.
.github/scripts/changed-scopes.sh  @9atatimer
# The checker itself: review-settled.yml sparse-checks out exactly these
# two files at tools_ref and runs them with statuses:write, so a change
# here changes every enrolled repo's merge verdict.
bin/review-settled      @9atatimer
lib/review-settled.mjs  @9atatimer
